We know that the force per unit length of a wire carrying current due to another parallel wire carrying current is given by

\begin{gathered}
\frac{d F}{d l}=\frac{\mu_0 i_1 i_2}{2 \pi d} \Rightarrow \mu_0=\frac{2 \pi d}{i_1 i_2} \frac{d F}{d l} \\
\therefore \quad\left[\mu_0\right]=\frac{[\mathrm{L}]}{\left[\mathrm{A}^2\right]} \cdot \frac{\left[\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}\right]}{[\mathrm{L}]} \\
\text { or }\left[\mu_0\right]=\left[\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}\right]\left[\mathrm{A}^{-2}\right]=\left[\mathrm{MLT}^{-2} \mathrm{~A}^{-2}\right] .
\end{gathered}

For a projectile to leave the gravitational field of the earth, its kinetic energy would be at least equal to its potential energy. That is

\frac{1}{2} m v_e^2=G \frac{M m}{R} \Rightarrow v_e=\sqrt{\frac{2 G M}{R}}

where $G$ is the universal gravitational constant, $M$ is the mass of the earth and $R$ is the radius of the earth. Hence the escape velocity of a projectile is independent of its own mass.

The temperature of the black body is

T_1=227^{\circ} \mathrm{C}=500 \mathrm{~K} \text {. }

$\therefore \quad$ Using Stefan's law, the rate of heat radiation per unit area per unit time is

E_1=\sigma T^4 \Rightarrow 20=\sigma(500)^4 \Rightarrow \sigma=\frac{20}{(500)^4} .

Now the temperature of the blackbody is raised to

T_2=727^{\circ} \mathrm{C}=1000 \mathrm{~K}

$\therefore \quad$ Rate of heat radiation per unit area

\begin{aligned}
E_2 & =\sigma T_2^4=\frac{20}{(500)^4} \times(1000)^4 \\
& =20 \times 2^4=320 \mathrm{cal} \mathrm{m}^{-2} \mathrm{~s}^{-1} .
\end{aligned}

